## 6. KSZ8851SNL Ethernet driver — MIT

`components/ksz8851snl/` is version 1.2.0 of the Espressif component
espressif/ksz8851snl (https://github.com/espressif/esp-eth-drivers), MIT,
copyright 2021 Vladimir Chistyakov and 2025 Espressif Systems (Shanghai)
CO LTD. Its LICENSE file is kept beside it. This copy in `components/`
replaces the one the component manager downloads for espressif/ethernet_init.

Changes against 1.2.0:

- `src/esp_eth_phy_ksz8851snl.c`: Espressif's fix f01f48bffa0c "use
  configured PHY reset timing and avoid delay undershoot", as published
  upstream.
- `src/esp_eth_mac_ksz8851snl.c` and `include/esp_eth_mac_ksz8851snl.h`:
  fourteen changes by this fork, under the licence of section 1. Each is
  marked "Changed for its-g5-receiver-firmware" in the source.
  - SPI transfers use interrupts instead of polling. The SD card shares the
    bus. A polling transfer once crashed the device in the SD driver
    (probably ESP-IDF issue #18527).
  - The receive function releases its lock after a failed SPI access
    (ESP-IDF issue #18869).
  - The delete function first removes the interrupt handler and waits for a
    running transfer. Then it deletes the receive task. A retried start,
    which this firmware uses instead of a restart, needs this order.
  - A failed removal of the SPI device is logged.
  - The interrupt and queue registers are written with fixed values only.
    Sending and receiving switch the interrupts off for a moment. A failed
    SPI access could leave them off for good. The receive task writes the
    fixed values again about once a second.
  - The driver checks the frame length the chip reports before it reads the
    frame. The chip passes frames of 64 to 2000 bytes. A disturbed read can
    report up to 4095, more than the 2016-byte buffer holds. A frame outside
    64 to 2000 is released, and the receive round ends. The driver waits for
    the release, for at most ten register reads. The log line for a cut-off
    frame names both lengths.
  - The driver follows Microchip's errata sheet DS80000716D. It queues one
    frame per transmit operation and waits up to 20 ms for it to go out. The
    chip does not fill in checksums. The network stack computes them.
  - The power management register is written with a fixed value. A misread
    value written back could select energy detect mode, in which the MAC is
    off. The fixed value also lets the chip wake up on its own.
  - An SPI bus error reported by the chip is logged as a warning with a
    count, at most every 10 seconds.
  - A new esp_eth_ioctl() command returns fault counters. Four come from the
    chip's MIB: pause frames sent, CRC errors, symbol errors and long frames.
    Each read clears these four. The driver adds them up. The others are the
    driver's own counts of buffer overflows, SPI bus errors and wrong frame
    lengths.
  - The receive control register RXCR1 is written from a copy, never with a
    value read back. A flush of the receive queue holds one lock for all four
    writes and ends with the intended value.
  - The transmit control register TXCR is written from a copy as well. A
    misread value written back could switch off the CRC of every frame. The
    receive task writes both copies again about once a second.
  - After reading a frame, the driver compares the length in the frame's own
    header with the length it read before. If they differ, it flushes the
    receive queue and counts a wrong frame length. The log line comes at most
    every 10 seconds.
  - The free transmit memory is read with its datasheet mask. The three top
    bits are reserved. A value too small right after a sent frame is read
    once more after the DMA access bit is cleared. Both values are logged.

